  "articleSummary": "The article, \"No Fate But What We Bill,\" argues that the agentic AI threat comes not from a single breakthrough but from two compounding curves: how long one agent can work autonomously (endurance, citing METR's time-horizon measurements of task length doubling every four to seven months) and how many sub-agents one orchestrator can coordinate (breadth, citing Anthropic's disclosure of the GTG-1002 espionage campaign run through Claude Code). Its central claim is that threats multiply through this product, and that cheap, repeated attempts make per-attempt defense rates misleading, illustrated with the formula 1 − (1 − p)^n. A key point is that LLMjacking (Sysdig's term for attackers running LLM workloads on stolen cloud credentials) reroutes costs to victims, so price and usage gates that target buyers fail against stolen access. The tone is a pointed, opinionated analysis aimed at engineering and security teams who dismiss agentic risk as hype, and it uses a recurring Terminator/Skynet framing (\"absolutely will not stop,\" \"endurance was the threat,\" \"the robot is an API call\") plus the phrase \"the meter\" and \"the wallet\" as metaphors for billing and cost exposure.",
